name: fresh pasta
yield: 500g flour = ~750g fresh pasta, 4-6 mains
notes: |
  One dough, many shapes. The default is northern-Italian egg pasta (sfoglia) — tagliatelle,
  pappardelle, lasagne, filled shapes. The eggless semolina variant is the southern one, and the
  right dough for anything you want chewy and firm, or that must survive a hard toss in a pan.

  Ratio. "4 eggs per 500g flour" is the folk version of the real rule, 1 egg per 100g — so 5, not
  4. Count is a bad unit anyway: a large AU egg is 50g in shell, ~44g out, so 4 is ~176g into 500g
  flour, ~35% hydration. That's dry. It comes together, and stiff dough laminates well, but by
  hand it fights you and cracks at the edges on thin passes. Weigh: 100g flour to 55-60g egg out
  of the shell, holding back the last 10% for flour and egg-size variation.

  Flour. Bakers flour is the wrong one — egg pasta wants extensibility, not strength. At 12-13%
  protein it springs back off the roller, needs a far longer rest, and eats rubbery. All purpose
  at 10-11% is closer to Italian 00 than bakers flour is, and makes genuinely good pasta with
  nothing bought.

  Worth buying is semola di grano duro rimacinata, for two reasons and the second matters more:
  it's the only flour for the eggless shapes below, and it's the correct dusting flour for
  everything here — coarse, doesn't hydrate, so cut strands sit in it, where soft flour turns to
  paste and welds a nest solid. Buy 00 as well for the textbook silky sfoglia, but that's the
  smaller upgrade. 50/50 00 and semola is a good middle: some bite, still rolls thin.
ingredients:
  - item: 00 flour
    mass: 500
    basis: true
    note: or all purpose. not bakers flour. 50/50 with semola rimacinata for more bite — 70:30 if you're hand-rolling, semolina stiffens the dough and fights the pin
  - item: eggs
    mass: 275
    qty: 5
    unit: ea
    note: weigh — 55g of egg out of the shell per 100g flour. ~6 large AU eggs to hit 275g
  - item: salt
    mass: 5
    optional: true
    note: 1%. some traditions salt the dough, some only the water. it slightly tightens the gluten
  - item: semola rimacinata
    note: for dusting, as much as needed. not optional in practice
components:
  - name: mix and knead
    steps:
      - mound the flour, make a wide well, beat the eggs in it with a fork and pull flour from the inner wall until it's too stiff to fork. a bowl works too — the well's only real advantage is that you can stop pulling flour in when the dough says so
      - don't discard the leftover flour, and don't force it all in either — work it in gradually over the first minutes of kneading and stop when the dough stops accepting it, usually with 5-10% left on the board. forcing the last of it in to use it up is the commonest way this goes wrong
      - the sticky paste stuck to the board is dough, not flour — scrape it up and knead it in. only the loose dry flour gets swept aside
      - "how to tell it's had enough: the board tells you, not the dough. early on the ball visibly hoovers up loose flour every time it touches the bench; when it's done the flour just sits there and the dough skids over it. clean board and clean hands means stop"
      - "palm test — press the ball into your palm and pull away. still wants flour: leaves residue. done: releases clean, faintly tacky. overshot: matte and floury rather than cool and smooth"
      - "fold test — fold the ball in half. fissures or cracks at the fold means too dry; smears and welds shut means it wants more. thumbprint should hold a smooth impression without cracking at the rim"
      - stop while it's still slightly tackier than you think it should be. flour keeps absorbing egg through the whole rest, so the dough you wrap isn't the dough you unwrap — it firms up noticeably. dough that feels perfect going in comes out stiff and crack-prone
      - err wet. too wet is fixed in ten seconds with a pinch of flour at any stage; too dry is near unfixable, because water won't re-enter a developed gluten network without turning it to a slippery mess
      - bring together, scrape the board clean, knead 10 mins
      - it should finish smooth, tight and firmer than bread dough, like firm plasticine. tacky — knead in flour a pinch at a time. won't clump after 2-3 mins of pressing — water by the teaspoon, not more egg
      - the knead is developing gluten and finishing hydration at once. under-kneaded dough tears on thin passes and no rest fixes it
      - wrap tightly, rest 30 mins minimum, an hour better. not optional — unrested dough springs back and never gets thin
      - keeps 24 hrs in the fridge. back to room temp before rolling or it cracks
  - name: rolling — machine
    steps:
      - cut into 4, keep the rest wrapped — dough skins over fast and a dried edge tears
      - flatten to roughly roller width, run through the widest setting
      - laminate — fold in thirds like a letter, turn 90° so the open ends feed in, run through the widest setting again. repeat 3-4 times
      - laminating is the skipped step that matters most: it squares up the sheet so you're not chasing a ragged oval, and aligns the gluten along the strand, which is what makes cooked pasta taut rather than floppy
      - then down one setting at a time, once per setting, no folding. dust with semolina if tacky
      - don't skip settings — a big jump stretches unevenly and you get thin patches that blow out
      - stop at 1-1.5mm for tagliatelle and pappardelle (setting 5-6 of 9 on a marcato or kitchenaid)
      - 0.8mm for filled shapes — two layers plus filling, thin enough to read the filling through
      - cut the sheet in half if it gets too long to handle
  - name: rolling — by hand
    notes: |
      Not just a machine sheet made slowly. The pin stretches in every direction rather than
      squeezing between two smooth cylinders, so the surface stays microscopically rough and
      porous and sauce grips it. Machine sheets come out burnished and shed sauce by comparison.
      That's the reason the sfogline in Bologna still won't touch a machine.

      Nearly all the difficulty is the last stretch from ~2mm to paper-thin. Anything thick —
      tonnarelli at 2.5mm, pici, lasagne — is a far easier introduction to the pin than a full
      thin sfoglia, so start there.

      Harder than it looks; the failure is a sheet thick in the middle. Expect several uneven
      ones. Uneven is fine to eat.
    steps:
      - a long thin pin (mattarello) if you have one — 3cm diameter, no handles, no bearings. a tapered pin works, a heavy handled one works badly
      - work on wood. the slight grip is what lets the sheet stretch instead of slide
      - flatten, then roll from the centre outward only, never back and forth across the whole sheet. rotate a sixth of a turn every few strokes to keep it round
      - once too big to roll flat, wrap-and-stretch. it's stretching, not flattening — roll the far third of the sheet up onto the pin, put both hands palm-down on the pin near the centre, then push the pin away from you (winding more sheet on) while simultaneously sliding your hands outward toward the pin's ends. the push lengthens, the outward sweep widens. that sweep is the whole technique
      - only needed for a sheet bigger than your arms can reach across. at 2.5mm tonnarelli off a quarter batch you'll never need it — just roll normally
      - unroll, turn 90°, repeat, alternating so it grows evenly both ways
      - dust with semolina, never soft flour — 00 hydrates on contact, turns to paste, absorbs into the sheet and leaves it gummy, and it quietly shifts your hydration. semolina stays granular and acts like ball bearings
      - dust only when it actually sticks. over-dusted, it slides instead of stretching. sparing while rolling, generous at cutting
      - thin enough to see your hand through — the shadow of your fingers, not the detail
      - work fast. a big thin sheet is drying the whole time, and leathery edges mean you've lost it
  - name: cutting
    steps:
      - dry the sheet on the bench 10-15 mins first — leathery, not tacky, or the strands stick the moment they're cut. but not brittle: if it cracks when folded the edges will shatter
      - dust both sides with semolina before folding or rolling it
      - machine — feed through the cutter attachment, catch the strands, toss straight into semolina
      - by hand — fold loosely into a flat roll or into thirds, slice across with a long sharp knife in one decisive stroke. sawing drags and fuses the edges
      - unfurl each cut immediately on the back of the knife and toss into semolina. strands left coiled weld within minutes
      - the bowl is for tossing, the tray is for storing. lift and separate with your fingers so every face gets coated, not just the outside of the clump
      - form loose nests, one portion each (~125g off a quarter batch), on a semolina-dusted tray — not touching, never stacked. a pile fuses at the bottom under its own weight no matter how well dusted; the semolina stops contact, not pressure
      - leave uncovered at room temp if cooking within the hour. a little surface drying helps. don't cover tightly — condensation is what ruins them
      - shake the excess semolina off each nest before it goes in the pot. loose raw semolina isn't the same as the gelatinised starch shed off the pasta — enough of it makes the water gluey and can leave a sauce faintly gritty. matters most where you're deliberately using a small volume of water, as in sauces/cacio-e-pepe
  - name: shapes from the egg sheet
    steps:
      - "tagliatelle 6-8mm — the default. ragù and butter sauces"
      - "fettuccine 5-6mm — same thing, Roman name, marginally narrower"
      - "pappardelle 2-3cm — hand cut, no machine goes this wide. wants a heavy chunky ragù"
      - "tagliolini/tajarin 2-3mm — roll the sheet to 0.8mm first or they cook to mush. butter, or broth"
      - "tonnarelli 2.5mm square — sheet AND width at 2.5mm. for cacio e pepe, see sauces/cacio-e-pepe"
      - "maltagliati — 'badly cut'. irregular diamonds off the offcuts. this is what scraps are for"
      - "lasagne — rectangles to fit the dish, no drying step, blanch 30s before layering"
      - "farfalle — 3x5cm rectangles with a fluted wheel, pinched hard in the middle. pinch on a slightly damp sheet or they spring open in the water"
      - "garganelli — 4cm squares rolled diagonally around a pencil over a ridged board"
  - name: variant — eggless semolina and water
    notes: |
      The southern dough: durum semolina, warm water, salt. Firmer, chewier, pale rather than
      golden, and it holds shape through a hard toss where egg pasta goes soft. The dough for
      hand-shaped pasta — orecchiette, cavatelli, pici, busiate, trofie — none of which want a
      roller at all. Also the correct dough under aglio e olio and other dried-pasta idioms.

      Cheaper, keeps better, and more forgiving than egg dough — no laminating, no thin sheet to
      tear. It just needs a long knead and a long rest, since durum hydrates slowly.
    ingredients:
      - item: semola di grano duro rimacinata
        mass: 500
        note: check the bag says rimacinata (twice-milled). plain "semola di grano duro" is often the coarse sandy grind — great for dusting, gritty and slow to hydrate in a dough
      - item: warm water
        mass: 250
        note: 50% hydration, ~40°C. warm speeds the hydration durum is slow at
      - item: salt
        mass: 5
    steps:
      - mix to a shaggy mass, knead 10-12 mins. it starts unpromisingly crumbly and comes good — don't add water for at least 5 mins
      - rest wrapped 30-60 mins, knead another 2 mins. noticeably smoother the second time
      - "orecchiette — 1.5cm rope, 1cm pieces, drag each across the board with a blunt knife, flip inside out over your thumb"
      - "cavatelli — same rope, drag with two fingers or a gnocchi board, don't invert"
      - "pici — 3-4mm ropes rolled by hand. thick, uneven, chewy, the most forgiving shape there is"
      - "busiate — wrap a rope around a skewer at an angle for a long spiral"
      - these cook 4-8 mins depending on thickness, far longer than egg pasta
  - name: variant — yolk-rich sfoglia (the fine-dining one)
    notes: |
      Richer, more tender, deep orange — for when the pasta is the point rather than the vehicle.
      Yolks bring fat and emulsifier and no water, so the dough is drier and more delicate: gentler
      hand on the pin, tears more easily on thin passes. At 0.6-0.8mm it's classic tajarin, served
      with nothing but butter and parmesan, or butter and shaved truffle.

      Most restaurant doughs sit between this and the default — whole eggs plus a few extra yolks
      buys most of the richness without the fragility.
    ingredients:
      - item: 00 flour
        mass: 500
        note: worth the real thing here
      - item: egg yolks
        mass: 300
        qty: 20
        unit: ea
        note: ~15-18g each. weigh, don't count
      - item: whole egg
        qty: 1
        unit: ea
        optional: true
        note: adds a little water back, meaningfully less fragile
      - item: olive oil
        qty: 1
        unit: tbsp
        optional: true
        note: helps it come together and roll without tearing
    steps:
      - as the default dough, but expect it to look hopelessly dry before it comes together — a full 5 mins of pressing before you add any water
      - rest an hour minimum. fat-rich dough relaxes slowly
      - "milder version — 3 whole eggs per 500g plus 6 extra yolks. ~80% of the effect, far easier to handle"
  - name: filled pasta
    steps:
      - roll to 0.8mm and work fast. this is the one case where you don't dry the sheet — it needs to stay tacky enough to seal
      - mounds of filling 3-4cm apart
      - mist lightly with water between the mounds, lay the top sheet over, press out from each mound to expel air before sealing the edges. trapped air bursts them in the water
      - "ravioli — square, fluted wheel"
      - "tortellini — 4cm squares, fold to a triangle, wrap the long edge around your finger, pinch the corners together"
      - "agnolotti del plin — pipe a continuous line of filling, fold over, pinch (plin) at intervals, cut between the pinches"
      - cook 3-4 mins. they float before they're done — floating is the air inside expanding, not the pasta cooking
  - name: drying and storing
    steps:
      - same day if you can, that's the point
      - fridge — nests on a semolina-dusted tray, loosely covered, 1-2 days. tacky and darkening after that
      - freeze — nests on a tray until solid, then bag, 1 month. cook from frozen, don't thaw, add ~1 min
      - fully dry — only the eggless dough, only thin shapes, 24-48 hrs on a rack until it snaps cleanly. home-dried egg pasta is a salmonella-adjacent gamble and it cracks
      - filled shapes freeze well and fridge badly — the filling weeps and the base goes soggy within hours
  - name: cooking
    steps:
      - big pot, hard rolling boil, 10g salt per litre. the water is the only seasoning the pasta itself gets
      - no oil in the water. it only coats the strands and stops sauce sticking later
      - 60-180 secs, not minutes. thin tagliolini can be done at 45 secs. taste from 45 secs and don't walk away
      - done when it's just past raw in the centre. fresh egg pasta has no real al dente stage — it goes from underdone to soft over about 20 seconds, so err early
      - the water goes starchy much faster than with dried pasta and emulsifies better. save a mugful before draining
      - finish in the sauce pan, not the bowl — 30 secs with a splash of that water and vigorous tossing off the heat is what makes sauce cling
      - it keeps absorbing after it leaves the pan. plate looser than looks right, serve immediately
